                <pb facs="0139" n="35" sameAs="frus:frus1890/pg35"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Mizner to Mr. Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1890-07-22"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 22, 1890</date>.
                        (Received July 26.)</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Mizner reports the great apprehension and danger existing in the city of
                    Guatemala, the rumored defeat of the Guatemalan army and of the nonarrival of
                    any answer to his communications for two weeks. He adds that Guatemala,
                    Nicaragua, and Costa Rica—Honduras consenting by telegraph—have signed a treaty
                    securing constitutional government in Salvador, and that they request the good
                    offices and moral support of the United States. He asks that instructions be
                    sent him by way of El Paso, Tex., and suggests the necessity for a United States
                    naval vessel in Central American waters.</p>
